-- Q1: easy / L2 — Section 351 property definition includes installment obligations
(6, 'Entity Formation and Restructuring', $EXPL$Hadley holds an installment obligation with a face value of $80,000 and a basis of $50,000 from a prior asset sale. Hadley transfers the installment obligation to a newly formed corporation for 100% of the stock. Under Section 351, how is this transfer treated?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Tax-free because installment obligations qualify as property", "Taxable because installment obligations are not property", "Tax-free only if the obligation has been held over one year", "Taxable as an installment sale disposition under Section 453B"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 351, installment obligations qualify as property for purposes of the exchange. When transferred to a controlled corporation solely for stock, no gain is recognized. Section 453B(d) specifically provides an exception for Section 351 transfers, treating them as nontaxable dispositions. Wrong (B): Installment obligations are considered property under Section 351; this is a common misconception. Wrong (C): There is no holding period requirement for property qualifying under Section 351. Wrong (D): Section 453B(d) exempts transfers to controlled corporations under Section 351 from the general disposition rules.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q2: easy / L1 — Section 351 control requirement definition
(6, 'Entity Formation and Restructuring', $EXPL$Under Section 351, what percentage of stock ownership constitutes "control" immediately after a property-for-stock exchange?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["50% of total voting power", "80% of total voting power and 80% of each class of nonvoting stock", "More than 50% of the total value of all classes of stock", "Two-thirds of total voting power"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): IRC Section 351 incorporates the Section 368(c) definition of control, requiring ownership of at least 80% of total combined voting power AND 80% of the total number of shares of each class of nonvoting stock immediately after the exchange. Wrong (A): The 50% threshold applies to Section 304 related corporation rules, not Section 351 formation control. Wrong (C): The value-based test applies to Section 332 subsidiary liquidation, not corporate formation. Wrong (D): A two-thirds threshold is not used in any IRC corporate formation provision.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q3: medium / L2 — Section 357(c) liabilities exceeding basis
(6, 'Entity Formation and Restructuring', $EXPL$During a Section 351 incorporation, the founding shareholder's only contributed asset has an adjusted basis of $50,000, and the corporation assumes a $75,000 mortgage attached to it. No other property or cash is transferred. Under Section 357(c), how much gain must the shareholder recognize?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No gain; liabilities are never boot", "$75,000 full liability amount", "$25,000 excess of liabilities over basis", "Gain equal to the asset's FMV"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 357(c), when liabilities assumed exceed the aggregate adjusted basis of all property transferred, the excess is recognized as gain. The $75,000 liability exceeds the $50,000 basis by $25,000, which must be recognized. Wrong (A): While Section 357(a) generally excludes liabilities from boot treatment, Section 357(c) overrides this when liabilities exceed aggregate basis. Wrong (B): Only the excess of liabilities over basis triggers gain, not the entire liability amount. Wrong (D): Section 357(c) measures gain by the excess of liabilities over basis, not by reference to fair market value.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q4: hard / L3 — Section 351 multi-party formation with services contributor
(6, 'Entity Formation and Restructuring', $EXPL$Dawson contributes equipment (basis $80,000, FMV $200,000) for 40% of a new corporation's stock, Elmore contributes cash of $200,000 for 40% of the stock, and Finch receives 20% of the stock solely for services to be rendered. Under Section 351, what is the tax consequence to Dawson?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No gain recognized because the transferors collectively own 100%", "Gain of $120,000 because the transferors do not have control", "No gain recognized because Dawson and Elmore together own 80%", "$120,000 recognized as ordinary income"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 351, Finch's stock received for services does not count as a property transfer, so Finch is excluded from the control group. However, Dawson and Elmore together own 80% (40% + 40%), meeting the 80% control threshold. Dawson recognizes no gain because no boot was received. Wrong (A): This incorrectly includes Finch in the control group; services are not property under Section 351. Wrong (B): The control test is met at exactly 80% without Finch; Dawson's gain is not recognized. Wrong (D): Even if gain were recognized, a Section 351 exchange produces capital gain, not ordinary income.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q5: easy / L1 — Section 721 general rule
(6, 'Entity Formation and Restructuring', $EXPL$Under Section 721, what is the general rule when a partner contributes property to a partnership in exchange for a partnership interest?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No gain or loss is recognized by the partner or the partnership", "Gain is recognized to the extent of FMV over basis", "The partnership recognizes gain but the partner does not", "Loss is recognized if the property has declined in value"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): IRC Section 721 provides that no gain or loss is recognized by a partnership or any of its partners when property is contributed in exchange for a partnership interest. The partner takes a carryover basis in the partnership interest. Wrong (B): Unlike Section 351 with boot, Section 721 has no boot concept triggering gain on formation. Wrong (C): Neither party recognizes gain under the general rule of Section 721. Wrong (D): No loss recognition is permitted on formation under Section 721, regardless of property value changes.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q6: medium / L2 — Section 362(a) corporate basis in contributed property
(6, 'Entity Formation and Restructuring', $EXPL$In a qualifying Section 351 exchange, the transferor's adjusted basis in the contributed asset was $120,000, and the transferor recognized $30,000 of gain due to cash boot received. Under Section 362(a), what basis does the receiving corporation take in the contributed asset?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$120,000 unadjusted carryover basis", "$150,000 carryover basis increased by recognized gain", "Fair market value of the asset at the transfer date", "$90,000 carryover basis reduced by boot paid"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 362(a), the corporation's basis in property received in a Section 351 exchange equals the transferor's adjusted basis ($120,000) plus any gain recognized by the transferor ($30,000). The corporation's basis is $120,000 + $30,000 = $150,000. Wrong (A): The unadjusted carryover basis must be increased by the gain recognized by the transferor under Section 362(a). Wrong (C): FMV basis applies to taxable asset purchases, not Section 351 exchanges where carryover basis rules apply. Wrong (D): The corporation's basis is increased by recognized gain, not reduced by boot; boot affects the transferor's stock basis under Section 358.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Section 358 stock basis after formation with boot
(6, 'Entity Formation and Restructuring', $EXPL$Kapoor contributes a building with a basis of $90,000 and FMV of $250,000 to a new corporation for 100% of the stock and $20,000 cash. Under Section 358, what is Kapoor's basis in the stock received?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$250,000", "$70,000", "$90,000", "$110,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 358, the transferor's basis in stock received equals the basis of property transferred ($90,000), decreased by boot received ($20,000), and increased by gain recognized ($20,000, the lesser of boot and realized gain of $160,000). Basis = $90,000 − $20,000 + $20,000 = $90,000. Wrong (A): FMV is the basis only in a taxable transaction, not a Section 351 exchange. Wrong (B): This subtracts boot but fails to add back the recognized gain, which is part of the Section 358 formula. Wrong (D): This incorrectly adds boot to the transferor's original basis without subtracting it first.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: hard / L4 — Section 338(h)(10) S corp target analysis
(6, 'Entity Formation and Restructuring', $EXPL$Buyer Corp acquires 100% of Target S Corp stock from its sole shareholder, Walsh, for $5 million. Target's assets have a total FMV of $5 million and an aggregate adjusted basis of $2 million. Walsh's stock basis is $1.8 million. Buyer and Walsh jointly elect Section 338(h)(10). Compared to a straight stock sale without the election, which statement best describes the tax impact on Walsh?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Walsh reports a $3.2 million capital gain under either approach", "Walsh reports $3 million of pass-through gain from the deemed asset sale instead of $3.2 million stock gain", "Walsh reports $3.2 million capital gain on the stock sale if no election is made, but the deemed asset sale gain of $3 million passes through on the final K-1", "Walsh always prefers the stock sale because it produces lower total tax"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 338(h)(10), the stock sale is disregarded and Target is treated as selling all assets at FMV. The deemed asset sale gain of $3 million ($5M FMV − $2M basis) passes through to Walsh on the final S corp K-1, increasing stock basis before the deemed liquidation. Without the election, Walsh reports $3.2 million capital gain ($5M − $1.8M basis). The election may be preferable depending on the character mix of the deemed sale gains. Wrong (A): The gain amounts differ—$3.2M on stock sale vs. $3M deemed asset sale pass-through. Wrong (B): This states the correct amounts but omits that the stock sale is disregarded under the election. Wrong (D): The election can produce lower overall tax when deemed sale gain is less than stock gain.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q9: medium / L3 — Partnership vs corporate formation: liability treatment
(6, 'Entity Formation and Restructuring', $EXPL$Martinez contributes property with a basis of $40,000 and FMV of $150,000, subject to a liability of $60,000, to a new entity for a 100% ownership interest. Under which entity type would Martinez recognize gain on this contribution?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A corporation under Section 351, because liabilities exceed basis", "A partnership under Section 721, because liabilities exceed basis", "Both entity types would require gain recognition", "Neither entity type would require gain recognition"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 357(c), when liabilities assumed ($60,000) exceed the aggregate basis of contributed property ($40,000) in a Section 351 corporate formation, the $20,000 excess is recognized as gain. In a partnership under Section 721, no gain results because the partner's share of partnership liabilities increases outside basis, avoiding the excess-over-basis problem. Wrong (B): Partnership formation under Section 721 generally does not trigger gain when liabilities exceed basis due to the basis-increase mechanism for shared liabilities. Wrong (C): Only the corporate formation triggers gain here. Wrong (D): The corporate formation does trigger gain under Section 357(c).$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q10: hard / L4 — Partial liquidation corporate contraction analysis
(6, 'Entity Formation and Restructuring', $EXPL$Westfield Corp has operated a manufacturing division and a consulting division for 12 years. Westfield sells the consulting division for $3 million and distributes the proceeds to its shareholders. Shareholder Tanaka, a noncorporate shareholder, receives $300,000 and has a stock basis of $180,000. The consulting division was acquired in a taxable purchase 3 years ago. Does this distribution qualify for exchange treatment under Section 302(b)(4)?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Yes, because both businesses were actively conducted for at least 5 years", "Yes, because the distribution is attributable to a genuine corporate contraction", "No, because the consulting division was acquired in a taxable transaction within the past 5 years", "No, because partial liquidation treatment is available only to corporate shareholders"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 302(b)(4) and Section 302(e), the terminated business must have been actively conducted for at least 5 years prior to the distribution AND must not have been acquired in a taxable transaction during that 5-year period. Although the consulting division was operated for 3 years, it was acquired in a taxable purchase, disqualifying it from the safe harbor. Wrong (A): The 5-year active conduct requirement is not met because the business was acquired taxably within 5 years. Wrong (B): A genuine corporate contraction alone is insufficient if the safe harbor conditions are not satisfied. Wrong (D): Partial liquidation under Section 302(b)(4) applies only to noncorporate shareholders, which Tanaka is.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q11: easy / L2 — Section 381 tax attribute carryover in subsidiary liquidation
(6, 'Entity Formation and Restructuring', $EXPL$Ridgeline Corp owns 100% of Peak Corp. Peak has $200,000 in NOL carryforwards and $50,000 of accumulated E&P. When Peak liquidates under Section 332, what happens to Peak's NOL carryforwards and E&P?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Both the NOLs and E&P are permanently lost", "The NOLs carry over to Ridgeline but E&P is eliminated", "Both the NOLs and E&P carry over to Ridgeline under Section 381", "The E&P carries over but the NOLs are subject to Section 382 limitations only"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 381, when a subsidiary liquidates under Section 332, its tax attributes—including NOL carryforwards, E&P, capital loss carryovers, and tax credit carryforwards—carry over to the parent corporation. Both attributes transfer automatically as part of the tax-free liquidation. Wrong (A): Tax attributes are not lost in a Section 332 liquidation; they transfer to the parent under Section 381. Wrong (B): E&P also carries over to the parent, not just NOLs. Wrong (D): While Section 382 may separately limit NOL usage after an ownership change, the carryover of both attributes is governed by Section 381.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q12: medium / L3 — Section 336 corporate-level gain in non-subsidiary liquidation
(6, 'Entity Formation and Restructuring', $EXPL$Thornton Corp liquidates and distributes land (basis $200,000, FMV $500,000) and equipment (basis $150,000, FMV $80,000) to its sole shareholder, who owns 60% of the stock. Under Section 336, what is the net gain or loss Thornton Corp recognizes on the distribution?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$300,000 gain; the $70,000 loss is disallowed", "$230,000 net gain on both assets", "$370,000 total gain with no loss offset", "$230,000 net gain after offsetting the equipment loss"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 336(a), a liquidating corporation recognizes gain or loss as if each asset were sold at FMV. Thornton recognizes $300,000 gain on land ($500,000 − $200,000) and a $70,000 loss on equipment ($80,000 − $150,000), for a net of $230,000. Since the shareholder owns less than 80%, Section 336(d)(3) does not apply, and related party loss rules under Section 336(d)(1) only limit contributed built-in loss property. Wrong (A): The loss is not automatically disallowed in a non-subsidiary liquidation unless specific Section 336(d)(1) conditions apply. Wrong (B): While the amount is correct, this choice lacks the specificity of D. Wrong (C): This incorrectly ignores the equipment loss.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q13: medium / L2 — Section 331 shareholder capital gain character
(6, 'Entity Formation and Restructuring', $EXPL$Palmer holds stock in Ridgeway Corp with a basis of $50,000 and a holding period of three years. Ridgeway completely liquidates, distributing property worth $120,000 to Palmer. Under Section 331, how is Palmer's $70,000 gain characterized?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Long-term capital gain", "Short-term capital gain", "Ordinary income", "Dividend income to the extent of E&P"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 331, amounts received in a complete liquidation are treated as received in exchange for the shareholder's stock. Since Palmer held the stock for more than one year, the $70,000 gain ($120,000 − $50,000) is long-term capital gain. Wrong (B): Short-term treatment applies only when stock is held one year or less; Palmer held for three years. Wrong (C): Liquidating distributions under Section 331 produce capital gain or loss, not ordinary income. Wrong (D): Complete liquidation distributions are treated as exchange proceeds under Section 331, not dividends, regardless of E&P.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q14: medium / L3 — Section 332 mandatory application
(6, 'Entity Formation and Restructuring', $EXPL$Regent Corp owns 85% of Cascade Corp's voting stock and 85% of its total value. Regent would prefer to recognize a loss on liquidating Cascade because Regent's stock basis exceeds Cascade's asset values. Under Section 332, can Regent elect Section 331 treatment instead?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Yes, because Section 332 is elective when the parent prefers loss recognition", "No, Section 332 is mandatory when the 80% ownership threshold is met", "Yes, if Regent reduces its ownership below 80% before the liquidation is complete", "No, but Regent may recognize the loss as a capital loss carryforward"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): IRC Section 332 is mandatory, not elective, when a parent corporation owns at least 80% of the subsidiary's voting power and value. Regent cannot choose Section 331 treatment to recognize a loss. The parent must take a carryover basis in assets under Section 334(b)(1). Wrong (A): Section 332 applies automatically when the ownership threshold is met; there is no election. Wrong (C): While reducing ownership below 80% before liquidation could avoid Section 332, this is a separate transaction with its own tax consequences, not a simple election. Wrong (D): Section 332 prevents loss recognition entirely; there is no loss to carry forward.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q15: hard / L4 — Section 336(d)(1) related party loss disallowance in liquidation
(6, 'Entity Formation and Restructuring', $EXPL$Keystone Corp liquidates and distributes two assets to its sole shareholder, Foster (who owns 60%). Asset 1: inventory contributed by Foster 2 years ago (basis $100,000, current FMV $60,000, built-in loss at contribution). Asset 2: equipment purchased by Keystone 8 years ago (basis $180,000, FMV $120,000). Under Section 336, what total loss may Keystone recognize?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$100,000 total loss on both assets", "$60,000 loss on equipment only", "$60,000 loss on equipment; $40,000 inventory loss disallowed", "No loss recognized on either asset"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 336(d)(1), loss is disallowed on distributions to a related person (50%+ owner) of property that was contributed with a built-in loss within 5 years before the liquidation. The inventory was contributed by Foster with a built-in loss 2 years ago, so the $40,000 loss is disallowed. The equipment loss of $60,000 ($120,000 − $180,000) is allowed because it was purchased by Keystone, not contributed by a related party. Wrong (A): This ignores the related party loss disallowance for the contributed inventory. Wrong (B): The amount is correct for equipment but fails to address the inventory treatment. Wrong (D): The equipment loss is not disallowed because it was not contributed property with a built-in loss.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q16: medium / L2 — Services for capital interest in partnership is taxable
(6, 'Entity Formation and Restructuring', $EXPL$Ortega receives a 25% capital interest in Greystone Partnership, valued at $60,000, solely in exchange for architectural design services. The other partners contributed cash and property. Under Section 721, what is the tax consequence to Ortega?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No gain or loss recognized by Ortega", "Capital gain of $60,000 reported by Ortega", "Ordinary income of $60,000 recognized by Ortega", "Ordinary income of $60,000 recognized by the partnership"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Receiving a capital interest in exchange for services is an exception to Section 721 nonrecognition. Under Treas. Reg. 1.721-1(b)(1), Ortega recognizes ordinary income equal to the FMV of the capital interest received ($60,000). Services are not "property" for purposes of Section 721. Wrong (A): Nonrecognition under Section 721 applies only to property contributions, not services. Wrong (B): The income is characterized as ordinary compensation income, not capital gain. Wrong (D): The income is recognized by Ortega the service provider, not by the partnership.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q17: medium / L3 — Section 351 loss nonrecognition
(6, 'Entity Formation and Restructuring', $EXPL$Duval transfers marketable securities with a basis of $200,000 and FMV of $130,000 to a newly formed corporation for 100% of the stock plus $10,000 cash. Duval has a realized loss of $60,000 on the exchange. Under Section 351, what amount of loss does Duval recognize?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No loss recognized", "$10,000 loss", "$60,000 loss", "$70,000 loss"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 351, no loss is ever recognized in a qualifying exchange, even when boot is received. Although Duval received $10,000 in cash boot and realized a $60,000 loss, the loss is deferred entirely. Boot triggers recognition only of gains, not losses, in a Section 351 transaction. Wrong (B): Boot triggers gain recognition but does not unlock loss recognition under Section 351. Wrong (C): The full realized loss is deferred regardless of boot received. Wrong (D): This incorrectly adds FMV decline and boot; losses are never recognized in Section 351 exchanges.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q18: easy / L1 — Section 721(b) investment partnership exception
(6, 'Entity Formation and Restructuring', $EXPL$Under Section 721(b), in what situation is gain recognized when property is contributed to a partnership?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["When the contributing partner receives a profits interest only", "When appreciated securities are contributed to an investment partnership", "When the contributed property has liabilities exceeding its basis", "When the partnership has more than 100 partners"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 721(b), gain is recognized when appreciated stocks, securities, or other investment assets are contributed to a partnership that would be treated as an investment company under Section 351(e). This prevents tax-free diversification of investment portfolios through partnership formation. Wrong (A): Receiving a profits interest for property generally qualifies for nonrecognition under Section 721. Wrong (C): Unlike corporate formations under Section 357(c), partnership contributions do not trigger gain when liabilities exceed basis. Wrong (D): The number of partners is irrelevant to gain recognition on formation under Section 721.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q19: easy / L1 — Section 336(d)(3) exception for distributions to 80% parent
(6, 'Entity Formation and Restructuring', $EXPL$Under Section 336, in which situation does a liquidating corporation NOT recognize gain or loss on the distribution of property?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Distribution to a 50% shareholder in a complete liquidation", "Distribution to any shareholder when the property has a built-in loss", "Distribution to an 80%-or-more parent corporation in a Section 332 liquidation", "Distribution of cash to any shareholder"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 337 (cross-referenced in Section 336(d)(3)), no gain or loss is recognized by a subsidiary corporation on distributions of property to an 80%-or-more parent corporation in a liquidation governed by Section 332. This preserves the tax-free nature of the subsidiary liquidation at both levels. Wrong (A): A 50% ownership level does not meet the Section 332 threshold; the corporation would recognize gain or loss under Section 336(a). Wrong (B): Built-in loss property generally triggers loss recognition under Section 336(a) unless a specific exception applies. Wrong (D): Cash distributions have no inherent gain or loss at the corporate level regardless of the ownership percentage.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q20: easy / L2 — Section 334(b)(1) parent carryover basis in subsidiary liquidation
(6, 'Entity Formation and Restructuring', $EXPL$Monarch Corp owns 100% of Shield Corp. Shield liquidates, distributing equipment with an adjusted basis of $250,000 and FMV of $400,000 to Monarch. Under Section 334(b)(1), what is Monarch's basis in the equipment received?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$400,000", "$150,000", "$325,000", "$250,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 334(b)(1), when a parent corporation receives property in a Section 332 subsidiary liquidation, the parent takes a carryover basis equal to the subsidiary's adjusted basis. Monarch's basis is $250,000, the same as Shield's basis before liquidation. Wrong (A): FMV basis applies under Section 334(a) to non-controlling shareholders in a Section 331 liquidation, not a Section 332 subsidiary liquidation. Wrong (B): This is the difference between FMV and basis, which represents unrealized gain, not a basis measure. Wrong (C): This is not a recognized basis calculation under any liquidation provision.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q21: easy / L1 — Section 721 no control requirement
(6, 'Entity Formation and Restructuring', $EXPL$What is a key difference between Section 351 corporate formations and Section 721 partnership formations regarding ownership requirements?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Section 351 requires 80% control; Section 721 has no control requirement", "Section 721 requires 80% control; Section 351 does not", "Both require 80% control immediately after the exchange", "Neither section imposes a control requirement"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 351, transferors must own at least 80% of the corporation's stock immediately after the exchange to qualify for nonrecognition. Section 721 has no such control requirement—any contribution of property for a partnership interest qualifies for nonrecognition regardless of the percentage received. Wrong (B): This reverses the rules; Section 351 imposes the control test, not Section 721. Wrong (C): Only Section 351 has a control requirement; Section 721 does not impose one. Wrong (D): Section 351 does require 80% control under the Section 368(c) definition.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q22: medium / L3 — Section 357(b) tax avoidance liability assumption
(6, 'Entity Formation and Restructuring', $EXPL$Warner transfers property with a basis of $100,000 and FMV of $300,000, subject to a $70,000 liability, to a new corporation for 100% of the stock. The IRS determines that the principal purpose of the liability assumption was tax avoidance. Under Section 357(b), what is the tax consequence?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No gain because liabilities do not exceed basis", "Gain of $70,000 because the entire liability is treated as boot", "Gain of $30,000 because only liabilities over basis trigger gain", "Gain of $200,000 equal to the realized gain"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 357(b), if the principal purpose of a liability assumption is tax avoidance or lacks a bona fide business purpose, the entire amount of liabilities assumed is treated as boot. Warner's realized gain is $200,000 ($300,000 − $100,000). Boot of $70,000 triggers gain recognition of $70,000, the lesser of boot and realized gain. Wrong (A): Under the general rule of Section 357(a), liabilities are not boot, but Section 357(b) overrides this when tax avoidance is the principal purpose. Wrong (C): The excess-over-basis calculation applies under Section 357(c), not the tax avoidance rule of Section 357(b) which treats all liabilities as boot. Wrong (D): Recognized gain is limited to boot received ($70,000), not the full realized gain of $200,000.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q23: hard / L4 — Section 332 vs 331 minority shareholder treatment
(6, 'Entity Formation and Restructuring', $EXPL$National Corp owns 90% and Lee owns 10% of Standard Corp stock. National's stock basis is $500,000 and Lee's basis is $80,000. Standard liquidates, distributing assets with aggregate FMV of $1,200,000 and aggregate basis of $700,000. Lee receives $120,000 in FMV of assets. Which statement correctly describes the tax treatment of National and Lee?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Both National and Lee recognize no gain under Section 332", "National recognizes $580,000 gain and Lee recognizes $40,000 gain, both under Section 331", "National recognizes no gain under Section 332; Lee recognizes $40,000 capital gain under Section 331", "National and Lee both recognize gain proportional to their ownership under Section 336"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 332, National (90% parent) recognizes no gain and takes a carryover basis in distributed assets. Lee, as a minority shareholder (below 80%), is treated under Section 331 and recognizes $40,000 capital gain ($120,000 FMV received minus $80,000 stock basis). Both treatments apply simultaneously in the same liquidation. Wrong (A): Section 332 nonrecognition applies only to the 80%+ parent; minority shareholders use Section 331. Wrong (B): National qualifies for Section 332 nonrecognition and cannot be taxed under Section 331. Wrong (D): Section 336 governs corporate-level gain, not shareholder-level treatment.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q24: medium / L3 — Section 351 stock basis when liabilities exceed basis (zero floor)
(6, 'Entity Formation and Restructuring', $EXPL$Burke transfers equipment with a basis of $45,000 and FMV of $180,000, subject to a $65,000 mortgage, to a new corporation for 100% of the stock. Under Section 357(c), Burke recognizes $20,000 of gain. Under Section 358, what is Burke's basis in the stock received?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$45,000", "$20,000", "$65,000", "Zero basis"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 358, the shareholder's basis in stock received starts with the basis of property transferred ($45,000), decreased by liabilities assumed ($65,000), and increased by gain recognized ($20,000). Calculation: $45,000 − $65,000 + $20,000 = $0. The basis cannot go below zero, and this formula produces exactly zero. Wrong (A): This is the original property basis without adjusting for liabilities and recognized gain. Wrong (B): This is the amount of gain recognized, not the resulting stock basis. Wrong (C): This is the liability amount, which has no direct relationship to the resulting stock basis.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q25: medium / L3 — Disguised sale under Section 707(a)(2)(B)
(6, 'Entity Formation and Restructuring', $EXPL$Vega contributes land with a basis of $100,000 and FMV of $400,000 to a partnership and receives a partnership interest. Three weeks later, the partnership distributes $350,000 in cash to Vega with no change in partnership operations. The IRS examines the transaction. Under Section 707(a)(2)(B), what is the most likely recharacterization?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A disguised sale of the land, with Vega recognizing $250,000 gain", "A tax-free contribution followed by a permissible cash distribution", "A bona fide loan from the partnership secured by Vega's interest", "A return of Vega's contributed capital under Section 731"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 707(a)(2)(B), when a contribution and a related distribution are part of a prearranged transaction, the transaction is recharacterized as a disguised sale. The close timing (three weeks) and large cash distribution strongly indicate a sale. Vega would recognize $250,000 gain ($350,000 amount realized minus $100,000 basis). Wrong (B): The proximity of the distribution to the contribution and its size indicate a prearranged transaction, not an independent distribution. Wrong (C): A bona fide loan requires repayment terms and a debtor-creditor relationship; the facts indicate a prearranged exchange, not a loan. Wrong (D): A return of capital under Section 731 requires an independent business purpose for the distribution, not a prearranged cash-out shortly after contribution.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3)
;

COMMIT;
